    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d332">

                <pb facs="0451" n="349" sameAs="frus:frus1927v03/pg349"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/4858: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Eberhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-05-21T11:00:00-05:45"><hi rendition="#italic">May 21, 1927—11
                                a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 2:04 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>139. Sacasa with 26 followers left last night for Puerto Limon on steamship <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Wawa</hi>. They may be expected to be joined by
                    Nicaraguan Liberals there and to combine in efforts to embarrass the Diaz
                        Government.<anchor n="75" xml:id="d332-f1"/></p>
                <p>Repeated to Costa Rica.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Eberhardt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d332-f1" n="75" xml:id="d332-f1-content">
                <p>By a decree issued at Puerto Cabezas, May 20, 1927, Dr. Sacasa announced the
                    termination of his regime, organized in that neighborhood Dec. 1, 1926 (file No.
                    817.00/4918).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
